run 8 - 2026-09-01 - the diagnosis channel worked and named the fault
exactly. Run 7 built clean and then died in the async package loader:
"Failed to load premade asset registry", then an assertion in BufferReader
on an IO thread. Build.bat compiles code; it does not cook content, and an
uncooked game binary crashes reading packages that were never produced.
Nothing was wrong with the C++. So this run cooks: RunUAT BuildCookRun,
cold and incremental timed separately, the packaged binary preferred for
the test because it is the one with content beside it, and the golden table
copied next to it because a staged build's ProjectDir is the staged tree.
The cook time is not a workaround, it is the half of the UE cycle that
Build.bat was never measuring.
run 9 - 2026-09-01 - the cook died in two seconds on "Editor target not
found!". Cooking is not a build step in Unreal, it is a commandlet run
inside the editor, so UAT refuses before it starts unless the project
declares an editor target; the project had only a game target. Added, with
the same Shared build environment for the same installed-engine reason.
-nocompileeditor is dropped with it: a C++ project cannot be cooked without
its editor module built, and that compile is real UE setup cost that
belongs in the cold cook number rather than being hidden by a flag that
makes the cook fail instead. Expect the cold cook to be the longest step
this probe has run.
