run 13 - 2026-09-01 - THE COOK WORKS. Run 12: cold 2.00 min, incremental
0.45 min, packaged binary produced. Gating the golden test on a switch was
the fix; it had been killing the cook commandlet it ran inside. The test
then did not run, for a new and smaller reason: a staged Windows build
ships two exes with the same name, a launcher stub at the archive root and
the real binary under Binaries\Win64, and newest-first took the stub. The
stub starts the real one and returns, so it exited 1 in two seconds having
written nothing, which reads exactly like the module failing to start. This
run prefers the binary under Binaries\Win64, prints both counts, copies the
golden table to four named places and looks for the result in four. It
should close the probe: build proven, port proven, cook measured, test
running against the packaged artifact.
run 14 - 2026-09-01 - TASK 007 STEP 1: the evidence channel on the UE side.
This is the question D1 actually turns on and it is not a performance one.
The probe now writes ue-verdict.txt with the commit on line 1, no spaces in
any value, whole-run numbers on one done line, and the words NO RUN when
nothing was measured. The workflow collects it by name, never by directory,
and guards it: the file is TRACKED, so a checkout restores the previous
run's copy, and a build step that died before rewriting it would commit that
older file under this run's message. Line 1 is checked for this commit as a
seven-character PREFIX, which is what the game was handed; comparing against
this repo's eight-character abbrev would have condemned every fresh verdict
and looked like a working guard. Both outcomes were run before shipping.
The success condition is small and exact: a committed file that
tools/verdict-read.py opens exactly as it opens the Unity one.
