run 16 - 2026-09-02 - TASK 007 STEPS 2 AND 3: THE FRAME. Step 1 proved a
traceable verdict lands; this is the still, and it is the piece the whole D1
comparison rests on, because an instrumented engine measured against an
uninstrumented one is worthless whichever way it lands. The capture is a
SEPARATE INVOCATION under -RenderOffScreen, never -nullrhi, which are opposite
things: nullrhi renders nothing and is correct for the golden test. It is a
SEPARATE STEP as well, because the build step measured 22040 characters
against a dispatch ceiling of 23184 and GitHub enforces that at dispatch, not
at commit. Two capture candidates ride this one run rather than two:
FScreenshotRequest first, HighResShot if the first writes nothing in 25s, and
the verdict names which one produced the file. A FILE-EXISTS CHECK IS NOT A
MEASUREMENT, so the binary decodes the PNG it is about to commit and prints
meanLuma over every pixel, min and max, non-black pixels over their
denominator, and distinct 5-bit colour buckets of 32768; one bucket or no
non-black pixel is BLANK and the step exits non-zero with the evidence still
committed. The maths and the done line live in FrameStats.h, which has no
Unreal type in it and is compiled and RUN by g++ here before dispatch: 25
checks, accepting case first, including the uniform grey frame that a
non-black check alone would pass. The frame's content is code-drawn debug
geometry and on-screen messages, two independent paths, because the probe has
no Content directory by design. UNRUN UNTIL THIS RUN: whether a packaged game
renders offscreen on that machine at all, whether either capture candidate
writes a file, and whether /Engine/Maps/Entry gives a world with a view.
run 17 - 2026-09-02 - PHASE B: THE STREET ITSELF, AND IT IS THE FIRST TIME
UNREAL RENDERS MERIDIAN. Every run before this photographed debug geometry in
an empty map to prove the capture path; this builds the 593-piece street out
of production/specs/vignette-pieces.json and takes the four named shots at
1280x720, the same four and the same size the Unity host takes. Nothing is
authored: every position, size and rotation comes out of that file, which
Ledger.Core wrote from the shared scene json, so a difference between a Unity
frame and one of these is a RENDERER difference by construction. The reader,
the median, the fov conversion, the gamma-to-linear conversion and every
printed string live in VignetteSpec.h, which has no Unreal type in it and is
compiled and RUN by g++ here before dispatch: 48 checks against the real
committed file, accepting case first, plus 30 for the pixel maths.
FOUR THINGS ARE UNRUN UNTIL THIS RUN AND EACH HAS A NAMED KEY. Whether the
engine's basic shapes reach a packaged build (DefaultGame.ini now asks for
/Engine/BasicShapes to be cooked; the emitter names the missing mesh if not).
Whether 593 spawned actors and 7 lights render offscreen at all. Whether
either capture candidate writes a file, which run 16 was dispatched to answer
and has not landed: candidate A is tried once and the run adopts B for all
four shots if it fails, so one dispatch answers it rather than four ceilings.
And whether the untextured frames are legible: the sky is black by design
here, materials and the HDRI are Phase C, and the verdict prints meanLuma per
shot so the first exposure series exists. The debug-geometry step stays in
front of it on purpose: if both fail the capture path is the suspect, if only
the vignette fails the street is.
The frame time is a MEDIAN of 24 engine frame deltas after 8 discarded, the
same counts the Unity host uses, and it is NOT the Unity number: Unity times
one Camera.Render and this times a whole engine frame. frameStat on every
shot line says which.
